  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 115 PM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nTuesday begins the long term period with a weakening of the monsoon \nplume. PWATs begin to retreat to the 0.80\" to 0.90\" range with a \nnotable downtick in afternoon convection. Mid-level dry air also \nbegins to intrude from the west further helping shut down \nprecipitation chances. Nevertheless, a few scattered afternoon \nthunderstorms will favor development over the south-central \nmountains near Ruidoso. Temperatures trend warmer over western NM \nrelative to Monday thanks to clearer skies, staying hot over eastern \nNM. The aforementioned mid-level dry air overtakes the state \nWednesday alongside a protruding portion of the H5 high westward \nover NM, bringing little hope for afternoon thunderstorms areawide. \n\nThereafter, the synoptic scale weather pattern across the western \nCONUS becomes a staging ground between three major competing weather \nfeatures. Numerical model guidance is depicting a deep 550dm H5 \nclosed low digging down the PacNW, while the H5 high slides back \nwestward toward KS, with an intensifying tropical cyclone chugging \nWNW thru the EPAC southwest of the Baja Peninsula. PWATs begin to \nclimb as a surge of monsoonal moisture returns to the Desert \nSouthwest late week into next week. The forecast challenge will be \nto what degree and where this plume will favor going precisely \nbetween these converging features.",
